# =============================================================================
# tensor-shape-generic — generic scaled_dot_product (shape-changing contracts)
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • Two `magnitudo` params (`B`, `D`) stand for rank-2 dims; the typechecker
#     computes symbolic result shapes through transpose, matmul, elementwise,
#     and softmax (`[B,D] → [D,B] → [B,B] → [B,D]`).
#   • The body uses tensor operators and methods (`transpone` / `matmul` /
#     `activatio_softmax`); elementwise multiply uses exact-shape `⊙`.
#   • One generic function replaces the concrete `scaled_dot_product_2x8`
#     overload pattern. Call-site implicit binding is Phase-1 machinery;
#     this exemplum proves the declaration (via `nota`), matching
#     `generic-shape.fab`.
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • Spelling a concrete `_2x8` overload instead of `magnitudo` params.
#   • Writing an explicit size argument without its marker (`sdpa<f32, 2, 8>(…)`):
#     a size argument carries `size`, as in `sdpa<f32, size 2, size 8>(…)`
#     (SEM057 size_marker_required).
#
# See also: tensor, magnitudo, functio, matmul
# =============================================================================

# Phase-2 payoff: one generic scaled_dot_product, operator/method-spelled,
# Depth-2 static body. Same shape as the unit-2A pin
# `generic_scaled_dot_product_method_body_typechecks`.

functio scaled_dot_product<magnitudo B, magnitudo D>(tensor<f32, [B, D]> qb, tensor<f32, [B, D]> kb, tensor<f32, [B, D]> vb, tensor<f32, [B, B]> scale) → tensor<f32, [B, D]> {
    sit tensor<f32, [D, B]> kt ← kb.transpone()
    sit tensor<f32, [B, B]> scores ← qb.matmul(kt)
    sit tensor<f32, [B, B]> scaled ← scores ⊙ scale
    sit tensor<f32, [B, B]> attn ← scaled.activatio_softmax()
    sit tensor<f32, [B, D]> context ← attn.matmul(vb)
    redde context
}

nota "shape-generic scaled_dot_product parata"
